description:
pkix-lint/src/lib.rs:671 — LintClone is declared pub and is the supertrait of Lint. The crate ships a blanket impl:

  impl<T: 'static + Lint + Clone> LintClone for T { fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn Lint> { ... } }

so any Lint impl that also derives Clone gets LintClone for free. This is the only legitimate use.

PROBLEM: Because LintClone is unsealed, a downstream crate can write:

  struct MyLint { /* not Clone */ }
  impl Lint for MyLint { ... }
  impl LintClone for MyLint {
      fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn Lint> {
          // Hand-rolled, returns a DIFFERENT lint than self
          Box::new(MyLint { ... different state ... })
      }
  }

This violates the implicit invariant that clone_box returns an instance equivalent to self. apply_parameter_overrides (lib.rs:1394) and the LintRunner clone-and-swap path depend on clone_box producing semantic equivalents. A hand-rolled implementation can silently corrupt the apply_parameter_overrides atomicity guarantee.

CALLER IMPACT: pkix-lint's stability contract around apply_parameter_overrides ("clone-and-swap; original runner unchanged on error" — see PKIX-7f92.19) is contingent on every Lint's clone_box being well-behaved. With LintClone public-and-unsealed, the maintainer cannot enforce this; a buggy downstream lint silently breaks the contract for that runner's other lints.

REMEDIATION: Seal LintClone via the standard "private supertrait of a private module" pattern:

  mod sealed {
      pub trait Sealed {}
      impl<T: 'static + Clone + super::Lint> Sealed for T {}
  }
  pub trait LintClone: sealed::Sealed {
      fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn Lint>;
  }

This keeps the blanket impl working for downstream Lint + Clone types but prevents hand-rolled clone_box from outside the crate.

Pre-1.0 free fix.
